---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Passkey">
  <h1>Passkey (WebAuthn)</h1>
  <p>
    Passkeys are a phishing-resistant first factor backed by
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/">WebAuthn</a>. Identity
    handles the server side of the ceremony with
    <code>github.com/go-webauthn/webauthn</code>. The browser does the
    actual public-key crypto via
    <code>navigator.credentials.create()</code> and
    <code>navigator.credentials.get()</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    The relying-party (RP) identifiers must match the domain users will
    sign in from, or browsers will refuse the ceremony.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID</code></td><td><code>example.com</code> — registrable domain, no scheme, no port</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_NAME</code></td><td><code>Example</code> — shown in the OS dialog</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN</code></td><td><code>https://app.example.com</code> — full origin including scheme</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_SIGNUP_ENABLED</code></td><td><code>true</code> (default) — gates passkey-first signup; set <code>false</code> to disable unauthenticated account creation via a passkey while keeping authenticated add-a-passkey registration</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Defaults (and the challenge-expiry knob) are in the
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration#passkeys-webauthn">Passkeys
    / WebAuthn section of the configuration reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Two distinct flows</h2>
  <p>
    There are two passkey enrolment paths, and they are not the same RPC:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Add a passkey</strong> (<code>BeginPasskeyRegistration</code> /
      <code>CompletePasskeyRegistration</code>) — adds a passkey to an account
      the caller is <em>already signed in to</em>. Requires a bearer token.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Passkey-first signup</strong> (<code>BeginPasskeySignup</code> /
      <code>CompletePasskeySignup</code>) — creates a <em>brand-new</em> account
      whose first credential is a passkey. Unauthenticated; no password, no
      prior session.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Add a passkey (authenticated)</h2>
  <p>
    The user must already be authenticated (e.g. they signed in with
    password). The flow is two RPCs.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Begin</h3>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/BeginPasskeyRegistration \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"deviceName":"Alice's MacBook"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "optionsJson": "{\\"challenge\\":...,\\"rp\\":{\\"id\\":\\"example.com\\",...}}",
  "challengeId": "ch_..."
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    The browser passes <code>options_json</code> straight to
    <code>navigator.credentials.create()</code> after JSON-parsing.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Complete</h3>
  <Code code={`const options = JSON.parse(beginResponse.options_json);
const credential = await navigator.credentials.create(options);

await fetch("/identity.v1.IdentityService/CompletePasskeyRegistration", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    challengeId: beginResponse.challenge_id,
    credentialJson: JSON.stringify(credential),
    deviceName: "Alice's MacBook",
  }),
});`} lang="javascript" />

  <h2>Passkey-first signup (unauthenticated)</h2>
  <p>
    Creates a new account directly from a passkey — no password is ever set.
    Control of the email is proven <em>in the same flow</em> with a 6-digit
    one-time code: <code>BeginPasskeySignup</code> emails the code, and
    <code>CompletePasskeySignup</code> requires it alongside the attestation.
    Because the code proves inbox control before anything is created, the
    account is created <strong>already verified</strong> — there is never an
    unverified account carrying a passkey for an attacker to hijack.
  </p>
  <Code code={`// 1. Begin — no Authorization header. This ALSO emails a 6-digit OTP.
const begin = await fetch("/identity.v1.IdentityService/BeginPasskeySignup", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email: "alice@example.com", deviceName: "Alice's MacBook" }),
}).then(r => r.json());

// 2. Browser creates the credential
const credential = await navigator.credentials.create(JSON.parse(begin.options_json));

// 3. Prompt the user for the 6-digit code from their inbox.
const otpCode = await promptUserForEmailedCode();

// 4. Complete — verifies the attestation AND the OTP, then creates the
//    (already-verified) account and returns a session.
const done = await fetch("/identity.v1.IdentityService/CompletePasskeySignup", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    challengeId: begin.challenge_id,
    credentialJson: JSON.stringify(credential),
    email: "alice@example.com",
    otpCode,
    deviceName: "Alice's MacBook",
  }),
}).then(r => r.json());  // { user, access_token, refresh_token, expires_in }`} lang="javascript" />
  <p>
    The server mints the new user id during <code>BeginPasskeySignup</code> and
    binds it as the WebAuthn user handle, so the credential created here can log
    in afterwards. The OTP reuses the same email-code machinery as passwordless
    login (TTL, attempt cap, and per-email send cooldown). Security properties
    baked into the flow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Email verified in-flow.</strong> The emailed code must be returned
      to <code>CompletePasskeySignup</code> before any account is created, so the
      new account is verified from the moment it exists. There is no
      unverified-account-with-a-passkey window — the account pre-hijacking
      scenario cannot arise at the source.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anti-takeover.</strong> If the email already has an account, the
      passkey is <em>not</em> attached to it — an unauthenticated caller can
      never bind a credential to someone else's account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Enumeration-safe.</strong> The OTP is sent (and the response
      looks identical) whether or not the address already has an account. The
      attestation and the OTP are checked before any account lookup, so an
      invalid code returns a single generic error that is the same for new and
      existing addresses — the endpoint never reveals which addresses exist. An
      already-registered address resolves to the same success-shaped decoy (and
      the owner gets a "someone tried to sign up" notice) with no passkey
      attached.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Disable the whole flow with <code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false</code>
    (returns <code>FailedPrecondition</code>); authenticated add-a-passkey
    registration is unaffected.
  </p>

  <h2>Login</h2>
  <p>
    No prior session is needed.
    <code>BeginPasskeyLogin</code> with an empty <code>email</code>
    permits discoverable credentials (the browser shows whichever passkey
    the user picks); supplying an email scopes the ceremony to that
    user's keys.
  </p>
  <Code code={`// 1. Ask Identity for options
const begin = await fetch("/identity.v1.IdentityService/BeginPasskeyLogin", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email: "" }),  // discoverable credentials
}).then(r => r.json());

// 2. Browser does the ceremony
const credential = await navigator.credentials.get(JSON.parse(begin.options_json));

// 3. Hand the assertion back to Identity
const complete = await fetch("/identity.v1.IdentityService/CompletePasskeyLogin", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    challengeId: begin.challenge_id,
    credentialJson: JSON.stringify(credential),
  }),
}).then(r => r.json());

// complete.access_token, complete.refresh_token, complete.user`} lang="javascript" />

  <h2>Managing passkeys</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ListPasskeys</code> — returns the credentials registered to the calling user, with <code>created_at</code> and <code>last_used_at</code>.</li>
    <li><code>DeletePasskey</code> — removes a credential by ID. Audit-logged.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Storage</h2>
  <p>
    Passkey credentials are relational rows in the datastore, linked to
    their owning <code>User</code>. The stored fields are the
    credential ID, the public key, the sign counter, and the user's
    chosen device label — never any private material.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
